      "writtenOn": "2022-10-07T16:42:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In place of calling `pm_device_state_get()`, you could have this callback implement the following:\n```\nswitch (action):\n    case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_RESUME:\n        pm_device_children_action_run(dev, PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON, NULL);\n    case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_SUSPEND:\n        pm_device_children_action_run(dev, PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF, NULL);\n    \n```\n\nMy understanding is that calling pm_device_runtime_get() triggers the power-domain callback with the RESUME action, and put() triggers the SUSPEND action.  The callback the tells all children to turn on or turn off.\n\nThe child nodes (temp sensors), could simply define a `bool domain_is_powered`.  The callback for each child will set or clear `domain_is_powered` based on the action PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON/OFF.\n\nThis likely uses more flash space, but then the temperature sensor doesn\u0027t have to know the specific power domain name.\n\nThe \"power-domain-g3\" can then be implemented as a separate driver file to make things a little cleaner.",
      "range": {
